<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: everyone does this</h2>
  <div class="callout sketch-first">
    <div class="callout-title">Sketch first: no words</div>
    <p>Sketch first, no words (5 min, alone): one drawing that shows the DIFFERENCE between weathering and erosion. Compare with a partner (3 min): can they tell which side is which without you explaining? Note it in your Field Notebook, dated; today's work sharpens exactly this distinction.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Two figures from Earle. In the first, salt crystals growing in the pores have broken this sandstone into a honeycomb, a process you can find on Chennai's coast. The second shows the same granite fresh (left) and weathered (right).</p>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img src="/assets/img/u2/u2-earle-5-1-5-honeycomb.jpg" alt="A flat sandstone surface covered in hundreds of small, closely packed round holes, like a honeycomb, with a rock hammer lying on it for scale.">
    <figcaption>Figure 5.1.5, honeycomb weathering of sandstone, Gabriola Island, B.C. © Steven Earle, <a href="https://opentextbc.ca/physicalgeology2ed/chapter/5-1-mechanical-weathering/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Physical Geology</em>, 2nd ed.</a>, CC BY 4.0.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img src="/assets/img/u2/u2-earle-5-2-1-granite.jpg" alt="Two close-ups of the same granitic rock side by side. Left, unweathered: glassy white and grey feldspar crystals with dark minerals. Right, weathered: the light minerals have turned dull and chalky, with the dark grains still scattered through.">
    <figcaption>Figure 5.2.1, unweathered (left) and weathered (right) surfaces of the same granitic rock. © Steven Earle, <a href="https://opentextbc.ca/physicalgeology2ed/chapter/5-2-chemical-weathering/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Physical Geology</em>, 2nd ed.</a>, CC BY 4.0.</figcaption>
